  "notes": "⛔ **The only major-mode trap entry in this batch, and that is the whole reason it exists.** The research calls it \"major-leaning and Mixolydian — unusually bright for the era\" with \"I–V–vi–IV-adjacent bright loops\", so the keys are spelled bare-major, the scales are `major`/`mixolydian`/`major_pentatonic`, and every progression family is in major numerals. ⚠ **`natural_minor` is deliberately absent from `scales`, and that is a decision rather than an omission.** The research says the bright loops appear \"as often as minor ones\"; a major numeral drawn over a minor scale reads as a *borrowed* chord in this engine — every bright loop would announce that it was leaving the key — so the minor side is carried by the `vi`-first families instead, which is the same sound arrived at honestly. ⚠ **Two things the research asks for that this model cannot say.** (1) \"Hook melody doubled in thirds\": the counter vocabulary has `octave_echo`, `arp` and `answer_lick` and no third-doubling, so the doubling is authored as an octave echo at the top weight. (2) \"The 808 sits under a synth bass\": `bass808.role` stays `bassline` because the 808 is the low end here (FR-007) and adding a second low voice would put two instruments on the same notes. What *is* authored from that sentence is the register — [20, 34] is higher and shorter than the Jeezy lane's, which is the \"less sub-dominant\" the research contrasts it with. ⚠ `confidence` is `medium`: the research says the era's bright-major variant is well attested but per-song production detail is thin. INFERRED: every velocity figure, the family weights, and the `snap`/`tambourine` perc density that stands in for \"snap-era percussion still present\". ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
